\subsection{Conformal Prediction in Robotics}

Conformal prediction (CP) provides distribution-free coverage guarantees for robotic safety and planning, including adaptive control barrier functions~\cite{zhou2025acp_cbf}, trajectory optimization constraints~\cite{wang2025cploop}, and teleoperation confidence calibration~\cite{zhao2024conformalized}.
We use CP scores as conditioning features for a learned correction module rather than as safety constraints, and find that at $K{=}10$, CP-derived features do not improve over observation-only conditioning (Section~\ref{sec:ablation}).

\subsection{Inference-Time Compute Scaling}

Test-time compute scaling improves language model outputs through best-of-N sampling with verifiers~\cite{snell2024scaling}, reward-guided verification~\cite{dai2025rover}, or decision-aware weighting~\cite{chun2025dasip}.
We transfer multi-sample generation to embodied action spaces; our decomposition reveals that learned correction, not selection, is the active mechanism (Section~\ref{sec:main_results}).

The aggregate improvement concentrates on medium-difficulty tasks where the VLA partially succeeds but makes correctable errors (e.g., chocolate\_pudding: 20\%$\to$60\%). Tasks where the base policy fails completely (alphabet\_soup: 0\%) remain uncorrectable. See Appendix~\ref{sec:per_task} for full per-task results.

